+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "`summary` tabulates the strongest paths at a node: the frequency span each covers over the sweep, its\n",
+    "peak power, and the output and source frequencies at that peak. `trace.dataset(node)` returns the same\n",
+    "data as an `xarray.Dataset` (`freq` and `power_dbm`, dims `sweep × tone`) for your own analysis.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**What this model does not do.** It is linear: no compression, amplifier harmonics or intermodulation\n",
+    "between tones. Spurs come only from mixers that have a `spur_table`. LO harmonics are generated once per\n",
+    "mixer. Mixer gain is looked up at the frequency the signal entered the system, as in xcvr's cascaded\n",
+    "analysis, so the desired path reproduces `System.gain` and `cascaded_gain` exactly. Set `spur_ref_power`\n",
+    "on a mixer (the input power its table was measured at) to make higher-order products track drive level.\n",
+    "Section 8 shows the checks that tell you when those limits are affecting a result."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "b97d5076",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## 8. Can I trust this trace? Four built-in checks\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The tracer is linear and works from the data you give it, so it also reports when a result leans on\n",
+    "something it cannot know. Each check is a property of the trace, so you can read it or test it in code.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**(a) The noise floor.** The dashed grey line on the spectrum plots in section 7 is the cascaded noise of the\n",
+    "*desired path*: kT at the source plus the noise figure and gain of every device up to that node (Friis),\n",
+    "integrated over a resolution bandwidth (`rbw=`, default 1 MHz). It follows the desired signal's\n",
+    "frequency, so it rides along the black arch. A spur sitting *below* it is buried in the channel's own\n",
+    "noise and rarely matters; a spur *above* it is a real contributor. Compare it with the previous plot:\n",
+    "the amplifiers raise the floor from node to node just as they raise the signal. It agrees with\n",
+    "`System.nf` and `System.gain` (this is tested)."
+   ]
+  },
